    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d720">

                <pb facs="0946" n="850" sameAs="frus:frus1898/pg850"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Stowe to Mr. Cridler.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Cape Town</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1898-05-26"><hi rendition="#italic">May 26,
                        1898</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 81.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith
                        copy<anchor n="3" xml:id="d720-f1"/> of the proclamation of Her Majesty’s
                    Government regarding the neutrality to be observed during the continuance of
                    hostilities between the United States and the Kingdom of Spain.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. G. Stewe</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">United States Consul-General.</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d720-f1" n="3" xml:id="d720-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed. The proclamation, after the usual preamble, quotes the rules in
                    regard to illegal enlistment and shipbuilding and the use of British ports by
                    vessels of belligerents which were published in the proclamation of neutrality
                    by Great Britain, see <ref target="#pg865">p. 865</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
